---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '@/components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const revised = '2026-10-10';

const pageTitle = 'APS Research';
const pageDescription =
  'The scholarly programme through which APS claims, methods, and applications are developed, compared, tested, and made available for critical assessment.';
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'APS Research' }]} />

    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>APS Research</h1>
      <div class="page-revised">Revised: {revised}</div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        APS Research is the scholarly programme through which APS claims,
        methods, and applications are developed, compared with existing work,
        tested against evidence and alternatives, and made available for
        critical assessment.
      </p>

      <p>
        It brings together peer-reviewed publications, preprints, deposited
        papers, completed APS Research, and substantive work in development
        where appropriate. Every item is identified by status so that
        completion, public availability, and external peer review are not
        confused.
      </p>

      <p>
        Research is the programme; the
        <a href="/publications/">Publications page</a> records its formally
        released scholarly outputs; and the
        <a href="/articles/">Articles collection</a> communicates the framework
        and its applications across the wider APS website.
      </p>

      <h2>Current Framework Status</h2>

      <p>
        APS is currently being investigated as a <strong>candidate general
        explanatory framework for biology</strong>. Controlled domain
        consolidation has established a coherent current substantive
        architecture comprising Cognition, Development &amp; Regeneration,
        Evolutionary Dynamics, Ecology, and Social Organisation, together with
        foundational commitments, cross-cutting resources, and interfaces.
      </p>

      <p>
        Subsequent cross-domain testing has not identified a genuine boundary
        or dependency inconsistency among the strongest cases examined, and no
        further substantive explanandum-centred domain is currently warranted.
        These findings support the internal and integrative coherence of the
        present APS architecture. They do not establish framework-wide
        empirical adequacy, explanatory superiority, or transfer of
        explanatory contribution between domains.
      </p>

      <p>
        Local research results therefore retain their own evidential scope and
        are not aggregated into framework-wide validation. Whether the present
        architecture provides explanatory value for biology as a whole beyond
        the organisation or redescription of already available biological
        explanations remains an open research question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Completed APS investigations have also produced restrictive findings.
        Comparative studies of biological agency and explanatory architecture
        have identified cases of equivalent redescription, local dispensability,
        or explanatory gain not established. Research into communication has
        not demonstrated a comparative advantage in reader understanding, while
        cross-domain reconciliation has established bounded architectural
        compatibility rather than explanatory superiority. These outcomes
        contribute to the assessment of APS by identifying where its claims
        remain unsupported, redundant, or in need of further testing.
      </p>

      <h2>APS as a Developing Intellectual System</h2>

      <p>
        The APS research programme is also documented as a process of conceptual
        construction, comparative testing, revision, consolidation, maintenance,
        and public claim calibration.
        <a href="/articles/aps-as-an-evolving-intellectual-system/">
          APS as an Evolving Intellectual System
        </a>
        examines how these activities relate while keeping explanatory,
        philosophical, scholarly, and developmental forms of warrant distinct.
      </p>

      <h2>Domain Architecture and Cross-Domain Research</h2>

      <p>
        Controlled corpus consolidation has established five current
        substantive APS domains: Cognition, Development &amp; Regeneration,
        Evolutionary Dynamics, Ecology, and Social Organisation. These domains
        are organised by explanatory function rather than by terminology,
        website classification, or separate levels of biological reality.
        Foundational commitments, cross-cutting conceptual and methodological
        resources, and boundary or interface populations can contribute across
        domains without thereby constituting additional peer domains.
      </p>

      <p>
        Subsequent cross-domain reconciliation examined the strongest apparent
        boundary and dependency tensions among the independently consolidated
        domains. No genuine inconsistency was established in the cases tested.
        In particular, an interface relation does not by itself establish an
        explanatory dependency, a causal dependency, or a universal
        prerequisite relation.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are architectural findings. They establish neither empirical
        confirmation of APS nor comparative explanatory superiority. They
        instead provide a more controlled basis for asking whether the
        resulting architecture contributes to biological explanation and, where
        explanatory contribution is claimed, whether that contribution survives
        comparison with the strongest relevant alternatives.
      </p>

      <h2>Earlier General-Biological-Explanation Coverage Assessment</h2>

      <p>
        The matrix below records an earlier framework-level assessment of APS
        research coverage and evidential status. Its seven coverage families
        belong to that general-biological-explanation assessment and should not
        be read as the current five-domain taxonomy of APS.
      </p>

      <p>
        The matrix remains useful because it distinguishes areas represented in
        the APS conceptual architecture from targets that had undergone
        controlled comparative investigation and from stronger explanatory
        claims requiring additional evidential support at the time of that
        assessment.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>How to read this matrix:</strong> Presence in the APS corpus
        indicates conceptual or architectural coverage, not demonstrated
        explanatory success. Controlled investigation records where a particular
        APS claim has undergone a bounded comparative test; it does not imply a
        positive result. Results apply only to the stated target. Explanatory
        contribution and transfer require separate evidential warrant and must
        not be inferred from the number, breadth, or interconnection of APS
        articles or research projects. The columns are distinct evidential
        categories, not stages through which a domain is expected to progress.
        The seven coverage families shown here were identified for the earlier
        APS general-biological-explanation assessment. They are not an
        exhaustive classification of biology and should not be conflated with
        the subsequently consolidated five-domain architecture.
      </p>

      <div style="display: flex; justify-content: center; width: 100%;">
        <a
