          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="aps-diagram"
          style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;"
        >
          <img
            src="/assets/diagrams/aps-research-coverage-evidential-status.png"
            alt="Earlier APS Research Coverage and Evidential Status matrix distinguishing public architectural coverage, controlled investigation, bounded research results, supported local explanatory contribution, and transfer or broader explanatory scope across seven coverage families."
            loading="lazy"
            style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;"
          />
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="figure-caption">
        <strong>Figure — Earlier APS Research Coverage and Evidential Status.</strong>
        The matrix records the evidential categories used in an earlier
        general-biological-explanation assessment. It distinguishes public
        architectural coverage from controlled investigation, bounded research
        results, supported local explanatory contribution, and warranted
        transfer or broader scope. The seven coverage families are not the
        subsequently consolidated five-domain APS taxonomy. Presence in the APS
        corpus does not establish explanatory success, and results apply only to
        their stated targets.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>No qualifying test record</strong> means that the earlier GBE
        assessment did not identify a controlled comparative test satisfying
        the evidential requirements used for this matrix. It does not mean that
        the area had not been researched, that APS made no claims concerning
        it, or that those claims had been shown to fail.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Not established</strong> records an evidential status; it does
        not mean refuted. Results shown here are target-bound and must not be
        aggregated across rows to infer framework-wide explanatory success or
        failure.
      </p>

      <h2>Current Major Research Programme</h2>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>General Explanatory Architecture</h3>

        <p>
          <strong>Status:</strong> Completed bounded assessment; further
          integrative and communicative investigation remains prospective.
        </p>

        <p>
          This programme asks whether APS can provide a sufficiently coherent,
          determinate, and testable explanatory architecture for biology as a
          whole while preserving differences among major biological explananda
          and avoiding causal, explanatory, temporal, or scale dependencies
          that the evidence does not establish.
        </p>

        <p>
          The investigation distinguishes three questions that must not be
          conflated: whether APS provides <strong>explanatory coherence</strong>,
          whether its architecture has <strong>integrative explanatory
          value</strong>, and whether it establishes <strong>comparative
          explanatory gain</strong>. The existence of a coherent architecture
          does not predetermine the answers to the latter questions.
        </p>

        <p>
          The completed assessment distinguishes architectural coherence from
          independently demonstrated explanatory or communicative improvement.
          Coherence and internal reconciliation do not themselves establish
          integrative explanatory gain. Any further comparison must identify
          a specific explanatory or communicative target, the strongest relevant
          alternatives, and an outcome capable of showing no APS advantage.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Featured Research</h2>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          Agency as the Defining Activity of Life: A Viability-Oriented
          Framework Integrating Process and Scale
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Peer-reviewed publication</p>

        <p>
          The foundational peer-reviewed presentation of APS as a
          viability-oriented framework for understanding life. It argues that
          biological agency is the defining activity through which living
          organisation sustains itself across process and scale.
        </p>

        <p>
          Spencer, R. D. (2026). <em>Biological Theory</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s13752-026-00547-6"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            DOI
          </a>
          ·
          <a href="https://rdcu.be/fkRpq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            SharedIt full-text access
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/articles/comparative-explanatory-methodology-theoretical-biology/">
            Comparative Explanatory Methodology in Theoretical Biology
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Establishes a common analytical protocol for comparing
          organisational research programmes through their explanatory
          targets, organising principles, explanatory priorities, conceptual
          architectures, domains of strength, and explanatory scope.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/research/auditing-dependency-claims-biological-explanation/">
            Auditing Dependency Claims in Biological Explanation: A Comparative
            Method and Three-Case Test
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Develops and tests a relation-sensitive, materially explicit
          procedure for assessing heterogeneous biological dependency claims.
          Its planarian positive result, <em>Pristionchus pacificus</em>
          transfer result, and glucose-homeostasis null test the method’s
          diagnostic capacity, transferability, and principled refusal of
          explanatory gain.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>Domain Consolidation and Cross-Domain Reconciliation</h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Consolidates the substantive APS corpora for Cognition, Development
          &amp; Regeneration, Evolutionary Dynamics, Ecology, and Social
          Organisation, then tests the strongest apparent boundary and
          dependency tensions among them. The research establishes a coherent
          current domain architecture without treating architectural coherence
          as empirical confirmation or comparative explanatory gain.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Publications and Preprints</h2>

      <p>
        The APS publication record includes peer-reviewed work, preprints, and
        scholarly papers deposited in recognised repositories. Each output is
        labelled according to its publication and review status.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/publications/">View APS Publications</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Completed APS Research</h2>

      <p>
        Completed APS Research presents substantial investigations developed
        to scholarly standards and made publicly available where an appropriate
        research object exists. Completion records the closure of a specified
        investigation, not necessarily a positive finding. Results may establish
        a bounded contribution, clarify an explanatory dependency, identify
        equivalent redescription or redundancy, or leave a proposed advantage
        unestablished. Completion does not imply external peer review,
        independent confirmation, or framework-wide validation.
      </p>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/articles/comparative-explanatory-methodology-theoretical-biology/">
            Comparative Explanatory Methodology in Theoretical Biology
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Develops a disciplined method for comparing biological research
          programmes without reducing their different explanatory priorities
          to a single theory or standard of explanation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/research/auditing-dependency-claims-biological-explanation/">
            Auditing Dependency Claims in Biological Explanation: A Comparative
            Method and Three-Case Test
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Applies a common dependency-hypothesis audit to planarian
          regeneration, <em>Pristionchus pacificus</em> mouth-form polyphenism,
          and mammalian glucose homeostasis. The differentiated positive,
          transfer, and null outcomes test whether the procedure changes
          defensible explanatory assessments without manufacturing improvement
          where established accounts are already sufficient.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>Domain Consolidation and Cross-Domain Reconciliation</h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Establishes and tests the current five-domain APS architecture through
          controlled corpus consolidation and subsequent cross-domain
          reconciliation. No genuine boundary or dependency inconsistency was
          established among the strongest cases examined, and no further
          substantive explanandum-centred domain is currently warranted. These
          conclusions are architectural and do not establish framework-wide
          empirical adequacy or explanatory superiority.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Research Standards</h2>

      <p>
        APS Research distinguishes established findings from APS
        methodological contributions. Claims are assessed through comparison
        with the strongest available explanations, attention to material
        evidence and inferential limits, consideration of alternatives and
        scope, and explicit failure conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conceptual coherence is not treated as empirical confirmation. APS
        earns scientific credibility only insofar as its explanatory
        connections can be identified, assessed, qualified, revised, or
        rejected.
      </p>

      <h2>Explore the Wider APS Platform</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/articles/">Articles</a> presents the wider explanatory
          corpus.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/">Publications</a> records formally released
          scholarly outputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/policies/citation-policy/">Citation Policy</a> explains how
          APS publications, research papers, and website resources should be
          cited.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The APS Framework is the research and explanatory platform through which these
        differentiated resources are organised and made publicly available.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>